Hi Cecile,

have you used the same set of permutations of class labels? If not, this might be a reason why the statistics differ slightly. There might be other reasons. But I do not know them cause I do not know samr.

The reason why samr and siggenes differ is that these are two independent implementations of SAM -- samr done by the developers of SAM themselves, and siggenes done by me years ago in my diploma thesis (the SAM version in siggenes has much improved since then). How to use SAM in siggenes is explained in the vignette to siggenes which you can obtain by calling

> vignette("siggenes")

and in the article also available in siggenes. The latest version of this vignette -- currently only available in the developmental branch of BioC, but will be part of the next BioC release -- also contains explanations for which arguments are important and which arguments of sam and d.stat should only be changed if completely understood and one wants, e.g., reproduce the results of a SAM analysis with samr.

Best,
Holger 

